{"name":"Lead Cast","hex":"#6c809c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lead","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy, pliable, inelastic metal element, having a bright, bluish color, but easily tarnished; both malleable and ductile, though with little tenacity. It is easily fusible, forms alloys with other metals, and is an ingredient of solder and type metal. Atomic number 82.","A plummet or mass of lead attached to a line, used in sounding depth at sea or to estimate velocity in knots."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover, fill, or affect with lead.","To place leads between the lines of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lead"},{"term":"cast","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move, or be moved, away.\n To throw. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","To throw. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of throwing.","The number rolled on a die when it is thrown."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lead","blocks":[{"text":"Lead is a chemical element with the symbol Pb and atomic number 82. It is a heavy metal, denser than most common materials. Lead is soft, malleable, and has a relatively low melting point. When freshly cut or melted, it appears shiny silvery with a bluish tint, but tarnishes to dull gray on exposure to air. Lead has the highest atomic number of any stable element, and three of its isotopes are endpoints of major nuclear decay chains of heavier elements."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Lead_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Lead_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead","wikidata":"Q708"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Angeln_zubehoer_grundblei_01.jpg/500px-Angeln_zubehoer_grundblei_01.jpg?utm_